1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,850
Hello everyone! This is Ria.

2
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:10,560
Today, I have a question for you all.

3
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:12,640
Let me ask you.

4
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:18,780
Here we have bread, noodles, and rice.

5
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:22,130
Bread, noodles, rice.

6
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:31,570
If you could only eat one of these for your whole life, which would you choose?

7
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:37,540
For me... wow... it's really hard to choose.

8
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:39,480
It's so difficult.

9
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:51,950
Today, we'll talk about bread, noodles, and rice, and at the end, I'll pick one of these three.

10
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:56,960
Or maybe you have already chosen?

11
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:04,420
Listen to the podcast until the end and try choosing one of the three.

12
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:06,160
Try choosing.

13
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:09,570
I went to a Japanese supermarket.

14
00:01:09,570 --> 00:01:10,980
I went to the supermarket.

15
00:01:10,980 --> 00:01:17,200
The first thing I bought there was bread.

16
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:24,260
I bought bread, then ramen, and finally rice.

17
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:25,600
Instant rice.

18
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:34,300
Rice that you heat up in the microwave like this is called instant rice.

19
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:35,510
Instant rice.

20
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:44,800
The most famous instant rice brand in Korea is called "Hetbahn."

21
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:46,910
It's very famous.

22
00:01:46,910 --> 00:01:58,160
So Koreans sometimes call it instant rice, and sometimes they call it Hetbahn.

23
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:02,880
Sometimes it's bothersome to cook rice at home.

24
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:10,880
Or when you're outside, like when having a barbecue, it's hard to cook rice.

25
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:13,530
At times like that, Koreans say,

26
00:02:13,530 --> 00:02:16,420
"Ah, should we just buy Hetbahn?"

27
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:17,920
"Should we buy Hetbahn?"

28
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:20,290
They say this a lot.

29
00:02:20,290 --> 00:02:26,220
But don't you get confused between rice grain and cooked rice?

30
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:28,580
Rice grain and cooked rice.

31
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:35,840
In Korean, "ssal" (쌀) means the grain before cooking.

32
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:37,260
Grain.

33
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:41,300
What happens when you cook this rice grain?

34
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:45,170
When you cook rice grain, it becomes cooked rice.

35
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:45,640
Cooked rice.

36
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:50,770
So what did I buy at the supermarket?

37
00:02:50,770 --> 00:02:52,540
Yes, I bought cooked rice.

38
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:53,830
Cooked rice.

39
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:57,250
Not rice grain, but cooked rice.

40
00:02:57,250 --> 00:02:58,730
Instant rice.

41
00:02:58,730 --> 00:03:04,960
I'm currently abroad, so cooking rice is a bit difficult.

42
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:08,330
So I bought instant rice.

43
00:03:08,330 --> 00:03:12,550
Because cooking rice is a bit hard, I bought instant rice.

44
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:17,060
Koreans really love rice.

45
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:24,370
No matter how many delicious desserts they eat, they don't feel full.

46
00:03:24,370 --> 00:03:26,220
They don't feel full.

47
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:28,750
They feel something is missing.

48
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:30,920
They feel something is missing.

49
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:35,370
Everyone, do you like Korean barbecue?

50
00:03:35,370 --> 00:03:42,950
Do you like barbecue with meat like pork belly and vegetables?

51
00:03:42,950 --> 00:03:47,600
My family also really loves eating meat!

52
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:55,180
But no matter how much meat they eat, rice is always necessary.

53
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:57,130
Rice is necessary.

54
00:03:57,130 --> 00:04:04,530
So when eating meat at a restaurant, they order rice in the middle.

55
00:04:04,530 --> 00:04:06,370
They order rice.

56
00:04:06,370 --> 00:04:10,500
They eat meat and rice together.

57
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:15,340
In Korean, there is a word called "bapsim."

58
00:04:15,340 --> 00:04:16,890
Bapsim.

59
00:04:16,890 --> 00:04:21,970
It means the strength you get from eating rice.

60
00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:23,180
Strength.

61
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:28,440
The phrase "Koreans are powered by rice" is famous.

62
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:34,960
So there are really many Korean expressions related to rice.

63
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:39,580
I'll make a video about these later.

64
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:44,570
Uh... my older sister especially loves rice.

65
00:04:44,570 --> 00:04:48,540
So when we travel abroad together,

66
00:04:48,540 --> 00:04:51,010
she says, "Ah, something feels empty."

67
00:04:51,010 --> 00:04:52,930
"I want to eat rice."

68
00:04:52,930 --> 00:04:54,170
"I miss rice..."

69
00:04:54,170 --> 00:04:57,280
She always says this.

70
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:01,200
I'm not to that extent.

71
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:04,030
Not to that extent.

72
00:05:04,030 --> 00:05:05,890
How about you?

73
00:05:05,890 --> 00:05:10,730
Do you feel a bit empty if you don't eat rice?

74
00:05:10,730 --> 00:05:16,410
Or are you okay even if you don't eat rice all day?

75
00:05:16,410 --> 00:05:17,270
Bread.

76
00:05:17,270 --> 00:05:19,840
Next is bread.

77
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:20,640
Bread.

78
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:25,370
When I first came to Japan, I was surprised.

79
00:05:25,370 --> 00:05:26,900
I was surprised.

80
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:32,200
I went to the supermarket and there were so many kinds of bread.

81
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:33,740
And they were cheap.

82
00:05:33,740 --> 00:05:42,520
They were so cheap that at first I doubted, "Is the bread not tasty?"

83
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:44,620
'Doubt.'

84
00:05:44,620 --> 00:05:46,550
I doubted it.

85
00:05:46,550 --> 00:05:52,110
But after buying and trying it, it was really delicious.

86
00:05:52,110 --> 00:05:54,470
Cheap and tasty.

87
00:05:54,470 --> 00:06:05,560
So honestly, I didn't eat bread often in Korea, but here I eat it a lot these days.

88
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:10,170
Especially I often eat bread for breakfast.

89
00:06:10,170 --> 00:06:13,070
I often eat bread in the morning.

90
00:06:13,070 --> 00:06:22,590
When I wake up in the morning, I'm so tired that honestly cooking is a bit bothersome.

91
00:06:22,590 --> 00:06:24,240
It's bothersome.

92
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:26,250
Right? (lol)

93
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:34,160
And in the morning, I don't have much appetite, so bread is better than rice.

94
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:38,140
Bread can be eaten simply.

95
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:41,770
You can eat it very simply.

96
00:06:41,770 --> 00:06:50,350
You can heat it in the oven or toaster... or sometimes just eat it as is.

97
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:54,830
I just unwrap the bread packaging and eat it.

98
00:06:54,830 --> 00:07:00,750
I usually eat bread or yogurt for breakfast.

99
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:07,760
But honestly, in Korea, bread feels a bit like a dessert.

100
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:18,340
After eating rice, when you feel a bit empty or want something sweet, you eat bread.

101
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:29,220
When I hang out with friends, after eating at a restaurant, we often eat a slice of cake at a cafe.

102
00:07:29,220 --> 00:07:30,900
Slice of cake.

103
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:33,340
But isn't that a bit funny?

104
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:38,890
Koreans keep saying they are hungry.

105
00:07:38,890 --> 00:07:46,020
Anyway, in Korea, bread is often eaten as a dessert like this.

106
00:07:46,020 --> 00:07:56,030
But just as rice is essential for Koreans, bread is essential in other countries.

107
00:07:56,030 --> 00:07:57,170
Right?

108
00:07:57,170 --> 00:08:00,480
You learned the word "essential" a while ago, right?

109
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:12,590
Among those watching this video now, I think many people eat bread more often than rice.

110
00:08:12,590 --> 00:08:14,510
How about bread, everyone?

111
00:08:14,510 --> 00:08:20,100
How do you manage to get by without rice?

112
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:22,410
How do you manage?

113
00:08:22,410 --> 00:08:31,910
Later, I'll also tell you about some delicious Korean bakeries for you all!

114
00:08:31,910 --> 00:08:33,630
Bakery.

115
00:08:33,630 --> 00:08:37,550
And lastly, noodles.

116
00:08:37,550 --> 00:08:38,590
Noodles.

117
00:08:38,590 --> 00:08:42,890
You all already know ramen well, right?

118
00:08:42,890 --> 00:08:47,210
Ramen is one of the noodle dishes.

119
00:08:47,210 --> 00:08:51,840
There are so many dishes made with noodles.

120
00:08:51,840 --> 00:08:52,960
Ramen.

121
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:54,100
Naengmyeon (cold noodles).

122
00:08:54,100 --> 00:08:55,370
Guksu (noodles).

123
00:08:55,370 --> 00:08:56,280
Udon.

124
00:08:56,280 --> 00:08:58,570
These kinds exist.

125
00:08:58,570 --> 00:09:00,870
Pasta!

126
00:09:00,870 --> 00:09:03,220
Pasta is also noodles.

127
00:09:03,220 --> 00:09:08,430
So in Korean, it's called "pasta-myeon" (pasta noodles).

128
00:09:08,430 --> 00:09:09,940
Pasta noodles.

129
00:09:09,940 --> 00:09:14,250
For example, "Shall we buy some pasta noodles?"

130
00:09:14,250 --> 00:09:16,270
"I need pasta noodles."

131
00:09:16,270 --> 00:09:18,520
We say like this.

132
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:27,220
I talked about rice and bread earlier, but actually my favorite is noodles.

133
00:09:27,220 --> 00:09:28,440
Favorite.

134
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:31,300
It means the thing you like the most.

135
00:09:31,300 --> 00:09:33,540
It has this meaning.

136
00:09:33,540 --> 00:09:38,960
Those who like Korean idols already know this expression, right?

137
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:40,140
Favorite.

138
00:09:40,140 --> 00:09:47,250
The word "favorite" is used a lot not only for celebrities but also in daily life.

139
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:51,230
Why do I like noodles?

140
00:09:51,230 --> 00:09:59,400
Because there are many kinds of noodles, you can make many different dishes.

141
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:06,080
Each noodle has a different texture, so eating them is fun.

142
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:08,320
Eating is fun.

143
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:16,430
My favorite Korean noodle dish is kalguksu.

144
00:10:16,430 --> 00:10:18,130
Kalguksu.

145
00:10:18,130 --> 00:10:22,970
It's noodles cut with a knife.

146
00:10:22,970 --> 00:10:26,730
This is not spicy at all.

147
00:10:26,730 --> 00:10:32,080
Not spicy, the broth is warm and delicious.

148
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:35,460
I think you will like it too.

149
00:10:35,460 --> 00:10:38,620
Please try it later!

150
00:10:38,620 --> 00:10:40,380
I recommend it.

151
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:41,870
Recommend!

152
00:10:41,870 --> 00:10:49,330
So today, I talked about these three: bread, rice, and noodles.

153
00:10:49,330 --> 00:10:57,430
If you had to eat only one of these for your whole life, what would you choose?

154
00:10:57,430 --> 00:11:02,330
As I said earlier, for me it's noodles.

155
00:11:02,330 --> 00:11:06,770
I think I wouldn't get tired of eating noodles for my whole life.

156
00:11:06,770 --> 00:11:08,650
I don't think I'd get tired of it.

157
00:11:08,650 --> 00:11:10,350
"To get tired of" vs "to be bored of."

158
00:11:10,350 --> 00:11:18,360
If you want to learn natural Korean expressions like these, please click the link!

159
00:11:18,360 --> 00:11:23,380
I have example sentences and audio files that I wrote myself.

160
00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:28,520
Then next time, let's talk about something more fun.

161
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:36,790
Please be sure to tell me in the comments which one you like among bread, rice, and noodles.

162
00:11:36,790 --> 00:11:41,520
Then eat delicious food today and stay strong!!

163
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:46,570
Have a nice day. Bye :)
